# The CSP line below is rewritten at build time by scripts/set-env.js. What is
# committed here is the fallback: Report-Only, with a wildcard img-src and
# connect-src, because the R2 media domain and the CFEdgeChat origin differ per
# deployment and a static file cannot know them.
#
# That fallback is deliberately weak-but-harmless. A wildcard connect-src gives
# up most of what a CSP is for — it is the directive that would stop an
# injected script posting stolen data to an attacker's server — so a real
# deployment should not stay on it. Supply all three origins and set-env.js
# substitutes the real hosts:
#
#   NG_APP_BACKEND_URL      the Django API        -> connect-src
#   EDGE_CHAT_URL           the CFEdgeChat worker -> connect-src (https + wss)
#   R2_PUBLIC_URL           the R2 public domain  -> img-src
#
# The latter two are spelled exactly as Django spells them, and take the same
# value, so the setting can be copied between dashboards as a whole line.
#
# With those set it stays Report-Only; add NG_APP_CSP_ENFORCE=1 to promote it
# to an enforcing Content-Security-Policy. Do that only after watching a real
# pass of the app report nothing: there is no report-uri, so violations land in
# each visitor's browser console, and the flows worth walking are Google
# sign-in, photo upload, chat, and a book page.
#
# Two CSP headers ship, and that is deliberate. The enforcing one carries only
# directives that cost nothing to turn on here — the app has no <object>, no
# cross-origin <form action>, its <base> is this origin, and X-Frame-Options
# already says what frame-ancestors says — so they need no verification pass
# and no opt-in. Without that, a deployment that never sets the flag would run
# with a policy that only ever watches. The Report-Only one carries the full
# policy, including the directives that would take the site down if they were
# wrong. NG_APP_CSP_ENFORCE=1 merges them into a single enforcing header.
#
# What the policy allows and why:
#   script-src   GSI (accounts.google.com) and GA (googletagmanager.com) are
#                injected at runtime.
#                No inline script exists to allow: the pre-paint theme snippet is
#                /theme-init.js, and angular.json turns off inlineCritical,
#                whose <link media="print" onload="this.media='all'"> was an
#                inline event handler.
#   'unsafe-inline' in style-src: Angular writes component styles into <style>
#                tags. Removing it needs build-side nonces, not a header edit.
#   accounts.google.com/gsi/style in style-src: GSI loads its button and
#                One Tap stylesheet from there.
#   fonts.*      the Noto family is loaded from Google Fonts in index.html.
#   img-src      book covers come through this origin's /api/cover proxy;
#                listing photos and avatars come from the R2 domain, and
#                Google account avatars from lh3.googleusercontent.com.
#   connect-src  the API origin, plus the chat worker over https and wss.
#   frame-src    GSI renders its button and One Tap in an iframe.
/*
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin-allow-popups
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains; preload
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: camera=(self), microphone=(), geolocation=(), payment=()
  Content-Security-Policy: object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'
  Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only: default-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; script-src 'self' https://accounts.google.com https://www.googletagmanager.com; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline' https://fonts.googleapis.com https://accounts.google.com/gsi/style; font-src 'self' https://fonts.gstatic.com data:; img-src 'self' data: blob: https:; connect-src 'self' https: wss:; frame-src https://accounts.google.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; manifest-src 'self'

# A year-long cache only for the files whose names carry a content hash.
# It used to sit on /* above, and every rule's headers apply to a matching
# request, so each SPA route (/tw/search, /tw/book, ...) was served as
# index.html with a year-long max-age. A browser holding such a copy after a
# deploy asked for chunks that no longer exist, got index.html back through
# the /* rewrite, and failed with "Expected a JavaScript module script but the
# server responded with text/html". Routes now get Pages' default
# revalidating cache; / and /index.html below say so explicitly.
/main-*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/polyfills-*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/chunk-*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/styles-*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/ngsw.json
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/ngsw-worker.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/theme-init.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/safety-worker.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/worker-basic.min.js
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/manifest.webmanifest
  Cache-Control: no-cache

/robots.txt
  Cache-Control: no-cache
